  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nFall-like temperatures persist through the weekend as winds remain \nnortheasterly behind Thursday night/early Friday morning's front. \nHighs in the mid-to-upper 70s can be expected for most locations \nSaturday, increasing to the upper 70s and low 80s by Sunday. Daily \nrain chances also persist this weekend, mainly in the Trans-Pecos, \nthe eastern and southern half of the Permian Basin, and in/around \nthe higher terrain (chances will be generally in the 20-50% range). \nMeanwhile, ridging starts to redevelop over western portions of the \nCONUS, and winds at the surface gradually become southeasterly again \nby early next week. Though the ridge will stay west of us, these \nfeatures nevertheless allow temperatures to continue to increase \nthroughout the Long Term period. By the middle part of the week, \nhighs in the low-to-mid 80s will have become more commonplace across \nthe area. For reference, these highs would be near/a couple of \ndegrees above normal by early October standards. Upslope flow and \ndisturbances in the flow aloft will continue to allow for low (10-\n30%) rain chances, mainly in and around the higher terrain south and \nwest of the Pecos River through next week.\n\nSprang",
